{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Behold, it is clouded</b> — his soul is haughty, like עֹפֶל and בָּחַן." "<b>it is not upright in him</b> — all the more so one more righteous than he. And the word לֹא serves on account of another [word]." "And likewise 'the righteous one — by his faith he shall live' — not from his hand. And the meaning: his soul is not upright in him, and he sees that the righteous one — by his faith he did not live from his hand, yet he himself shall live and escape. And the meaning, by way of wonder: behold, he sees that the righteous one, on account of his faith, does not escape from his hand — how does he not consider about himself that his soul is clouded and not upright, and he is 'a man of wine, a traitor and an arrogant man' — who has done all these decrees and evils? And how does he not consider about himself that the parable is taken up against him, and its singling-out is riddles hidden from him by way of allegory, and it is explained well, as written: 'Woe to him who increases' and all that is written after it."], :book :habakkuk, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["<b>Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk</b>- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"] ["Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:49:11.719170438Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}